  "query_preview": "..\n\nA surface cold front associated with the northeast Canada low \npressure system will work through Southeast Michigan between 19-01z \ntoday. Moisture progs in the plan view continue to suggest that \ndeeper thetae content and saturation resides in an extremely narrow \nribbon that is only approximately a few tens of miles across. The \nlimited moisture will significantly limit the coverage of showers and \nisolated thunderstorms. Forecast soundings show residual stability \nholding with the midlevel dry air only becoming conditionally \nunstable with the arrival of the aforementioned moisture/saturation \nbetween 3.0 and 10.0 kft agl. MUCAPE is projected to reach 200 J/kg \nMUCAPE. A few embedded non-severe thunderstorms will be possible \nbefore 01z.\n\nStrongly increasing anticyclonic flow trajectories with notable \nanticyclonic hemispheric scale roll to the height field will lead to \nvery stable weather Friday and Saturday. High confidence that \nsurface ridging will hold on right through the weekend resulting in \ndry weather. An inverted troughing feature into portions of northern \nIllinois and as close as the southern rim of Lake Michigan may \nprovide for a good amount of cloud immediately upstream and even \nover Southeast Michigan Saturday. Incredible stretch of good Autumn \nweather continues with highs in the 60s through Sunday, some 7 to 12 \ndegrees above normal.\n\nUncertainty increases for the beginning of next week with regards to \ntiming of shortwave energy. Antecedent dry air will limit widespread \nprecipitation early Monday as a secondary surge in warm air \nadvection occurs. By late Monday and early Tuesday, timing and \napproach of the longwave trough suggests southerly flow will be on \nthe increase as well as the low level jet. Clouds are undoubtedly a \nhuge influence on temperatures but given projections of the ridge \nstructure, highs reaching 70 early next week is a definite \npossibility. The current forecast begins to ramp up PoPs early \nTuesday and that seems adequate. No significant wind episode is \nanticipated in the next 7 days.",
